{"availability":"available","full":"danskyvich/fire-tracker","name":"fire-tracker","description":"A web application that tracks real-life wildfire occurrences across the world.","category":"Developer tools","language":"TypeScript","stars":1,"forks":0,"created":"2026-09-08T10:27:30Z","updated":"2026-10-08T16:08:55Z","license":"Apache-2.0","branch":"main","homepage":"https://fire-tracker-dpjrs.vercel.app/","topics":["nasa-api","net","nextjs","tracker-application","wildfire","world-map"],"demo":"https://fire-tracker-dpjrs.vercel.app/","color":"#193546","ink":"#a6d6e8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-18T00:45:53.099Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-18T00:45:53.099Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-18T00:45:53.099Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-08T23:43:42.706Z","stars":1,"forks":0},{"at":"2026-10-11T22:37:49.764Z","stars":1,"forks":0},{"at":"2026-10-18T00:45:53.099Z","stars":1,"forks":0}],"technologies":["Next.js"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["A simple web application that displays all possible fire instances (or hotspots) based on several parameters. Data is fetched from several sources such as fire data from NASA FIRMS and World Air Quality Index from AQICN.com.","The basemap is rendered using CARTO Basemap (\"Dark matter\" variant) and Maplibre-gl. Deck.gl was utilized for rendering the fire hotspots overlay, and Redis was used for caching existing data and prevent unnecessary API fetches. The backend utilizes the ASP.NET environment for writing server-side operations such as API requests, and redis caching."],"features":["Map interaction: Zoom in and out.","Geolocation: Users can see where they are (if they consented) on the map using native navigator.geolocation API.","Responsiveness on smaller screens (for mobile devices, etc.)","View current fire hotspots around the globe, and view data on each of them. The data includes:","confidence → low/nominal/high."],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/danskyvich/fire-tracker/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"7c1cc3ad57c